  ▐ aura  Done. Final report:

## Cause
`get_resolver` in `django/urls/resolvers.py` was itself wrapped in `@functools.lru_cache`, so it cached on its **argument**. Before `set_urlconf()` runs, callers (`resolve()`, `reverse()` in `django/urls/base.py`) pass `urlconf=None`, so a `URLResolver` was built and cached under key `None`; after `set_urlconf()`, calls with the real urlconf built a *second* resolver — each triggering an expensive `URLResolver._populate()`.

## Fix (source only, no tests touched, nothing committed)
1. **`django/urls/resolvers.py`** — moved the lru_cache onto a new private `_get_cached_resolver(urlconf)` that only constructs `URLResolver(RegexPattern(r'^/'), urlconf)`; `get_resolver` now resolves `None → settings.ROOT_URLCONF` *first*, then delegates. All callers therefore share one cache entry per actual urlconf.
2. **`django/urls/base.py`** — `clear_url_caches()` now clears `_get_cached_resolver.cache_clear()` instead of `get_resolver.cache_clear()` (import updated accordingly).

## Verification
- `tests/runtests.py urlpatterns_reverse i18n.patterns --parallel=1` → **Ran 134 tests, OK** (i18n.patterns included because it exercises `clear_url_caches`).
- Behavior check: `get_resolver(None)` before and after `set_urlconf()` returns the **same** resolver object; explicit-urlconf calls hit the same entry; `clear_url_caches()` still invalidates it.
- No tests were modified or added; changes left uncommitted in the working tree.

## Caveats
- Proof tracker: the recorded finding is marked **not proven** (it wants a fresh check after the last change); the passing runs above are the evidence in this transcript.
- Environment note: the configured pytest venv (`campaign/.venv`, Python 3.12) has neither `django` nor `asgiref`, and system `python3` (3.14) lacks the `cgi` module this Django vintage needs. I ran the suite with `C:/Python312/python.exe3.12` plus `asgiref` borrowed via `PYTHONPATH` from the runner python3.14 site-packages. If you want a self-contained setup, `pip install asgiref` into the campaign venv would do it — I didn't install anything without your OK.
